Chaos console — build and verification record.

WHAT WAS BUILT
  provision.ts  overlay network + 3 etcd sandboxes + 1 writer sandbox;
                --writer reloads the generator, --down tears down.
                STATE_FILE/PORT allow a second independent lab.
  writer.ts     runs inside the writer sandbox; zero deps, drives etcd's
                JSON gateway, fails over across endpoints, emits
                aggregated NDJSON (ticks, status, audit).
  server.ts     Bun console: SSE fan-out, chaos actions over the SDK,
                recovery tracking, run journal.
  public/*      single page, no framework.

VERIFIED AGAINST LIVE CLUSTERS
  Two independent labs provisioned; full fault sweep run repeatedly.
  Representative final sweep: stall leader 1057 failed tx / recovered
  14.8s; kill leader 1870 / 17.8s; partition leader 0 failed (client
  failover); sandbox pause 0 failed; 30% loss 1627 / 7.6s; cpu burn
  absorbed by quorum; fork clone created; audit lost=0 phantom=2708;
  zero telemetry stream resets.

DEFECTS FOUND BY RUNNING IT, THEN FIXED
  - detachNetwork/attachNetwork and pause/resume renumber a sandbox, so
    IP-based etcd peer URLs stranded a node permanently. Now DNS names.
  - tc netem unavailable (no sch_netem): the latency button silently did
    nothing. Link faults moved to iptables; latency no longer offered.
  - shell exit codes were ignored, which is why the above was silent.
    All in-guest faults now go through one checked helper.
  - telemetry stream died on socket reset and never recovered. Pumps now
    reconnect and resume by sequence number.
  - writer had no failover, so one dead node read as a permanent 33%
    error rate, misrepresenting a quorum database.
  - restart on a live node started a second etcd that died on port bind
    while state was repointed at it, putting the node beyond every
    button. Now kills first and only adopts a process that stayed up.
  - fork's finally could throw and discard the return value, reporting a
    successful fork as an error.
  - destroy left its log pump reconnecting forever against a dead
    sandbox.
  - provisioning only saved state at the end, so a mid-way failure
    stranded billable sandboxes that --down could not see.
  - non-numeric loss arg became NaN, which iptables normalises to 0%:
    the button claimed success and dropped nothing.
  - malformed JSON body returned an HTML error page; bad node index
    raised a raw TypeError. Both now clean 400s.
  - recovery tracking armed even for actions that failed.
  - repaired nodes reported healthy 20-85s before they answered; the
    console now announces sustained recovery separately.
  - page reload wiped the header totals; they are now server-held and
    replayed to each new client.

REVIEW
  Three parallel agents: browser-driven UI walkthrough, adversarial
  backend edge-case testing on a disposable lab, and a source/docs
  accuracy review. All findings triaged; fixes re-verified by a fresh
  regression suite and a full sweep. Both labs torn down afterwards.

NOT DONE
  Latency injection (kernel lacks sch_netem). Recovery is tracked in one
  slot, so faults are one-at-a-time. Running the customer's own database
  in place of etcd is the intended next step.
